      },
      "worked": "Since $\\log_2(y)$ is defined, $y>0$; then $xy=40$ gives $x>0$. Let $u=\\log_2(x)$ and $v=\\log_2(y)$. Taking logarithms to base 2 of both equations gives\n$$\\begin{align*}u+v&=\\log_2(40)\\\\uv&=\\log_2(100).\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $v=\\log_2(40)-u$:\n$$\\begin{align*}u\\bigl(\\log_2(40)-u\\bigr)&=\\log_2(100)\\\\u^2-(2+\\log_2(10))u+2\\log_2(10)&=0\\\\(u-2)(u-\\log_2(10))&=0\\\\u&=2\\text{ or }\\log_2(10)\\\\x&=4\\text{ or }10.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing $y=\\frac{40}{x}$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(x,y)&=(4,10)\\text{ or }(10,4).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth pairs satisfy the original equations.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
